---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import { STAGED_RELEASE_ADR } from "../../lib/adr-reference";
import { adrPath, getAdrs, requireAdr } from "../../lib/adrs";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const stagedReleaseAdr = requireAdr(await getAdrs(), STAGED_RELEASE_ADR);
---

<DocLayout
  title="Releases & licenses"
  eyebrow="SECTION 11 / DISTRIBUTION"
  description="Automated, reproducible releases deliver crates, Linux archives, checksums, and this manual."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Release channels</h2><table class="spec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Artifact</th></tr></thead><tbody
        ><tr
          ><td>crates.io</td><td
            ><code>lanyard-ssh-agent</code> source package</td
          ></tr
        ><tr
          ><td>GitHub Releases</td><td
            >x86_64 and aarch64 Linux archives plus checksums</td
          ></tr
        ><tr
          ><td>Nix flake</td><td
            >package, app, checks, and Home Manager module</td
          ></tr
        ><tr><td>GitHub Pages</td><td>This versioned operator manual</td></tr
        ></tbody
      >
    </table>
    <h2>Versioning</h2><p>
      Every main revision must pass the full repository check before the
      release state machine can start. Release-plz then derives changelog
      entries and versions from Conventional Commits, and the trunk-based
      workflow signs the release commit and tag. Cargo-dist builds platform
      artifacts from that exact tag commit.
      Third-party actions are pinned to immutable revisions so changes to the
      release machinery are reviewable.
    </p>
    <h2>Publication order</h2><ol>
      <li>Build and checksum both Linux archives.</li><li>
        Upload the verified files to a draft GitHub Release.
      </li><li>Publish the matching source package to crates.io.</li><li>
        Make the GitHub Release public only after the registry confirms the
        version.
      </li>
    </ol><p>
      No release pull request or second release workflow is involved. Safe
      retries reuse the draft and the existing signed tag; inconsistent state
      fails closed. Rapid pushes can coalesce into one natural release, but
      unverified code is never substituted for the revision that passed.
    </p>
    <h2>
      Verify a download
    </h2><pre><code>sha256sum --check lanyard-ssh-agent-*.sha256</code></pre>
    <h2>Dual license</h2><p>
      Unless explicitly stated otherwise, Lanyard is available under either the
      Apache License, Version 2.0 or the MIT license, at your option.
      Contributions are accepted under the same terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${base}${adrPath(stagedReleaseAdr)}`}
        >Read the staged-release decision →</a
      ><br />
      <a href="https://github.com/jwilger/lanyard-ssh-agent/releases"
        >Browse releases →</a
      >
    </p>
  </div>
</DocLayout>
